<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis VII of France</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1120">1120</a>–<a href="/years/1180">1180</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Louis the Young · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-dynasty">Capetian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis VII was King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> from 1137 to 1180, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-dynasty">Capetian dynasty</a>. His marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-aquitaine">Eleanor of Aquitaine</a> brought the vast duchy of Aquitaine under the crown, but after he led the unsuccessful Second Crusade the marriage was annulled in 1152, and Eleanor married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II of England</a>. His long rivalry with the Angevins shaped his reign, while royal authority around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> grew.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-vii-of-france.jpg" alt="Louis VII of France" width="320" height="323" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Natalis de Wailly (1805-1886) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_7.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They also faced the dukes of Aquitaine, who claimed Toulouse by marriage: William IX occupied the city twice, and in 1159 Henry II of England besieged it until <b>Louis VII of France</b> entered Toulouse to support Raymond V and Henry withdrew.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">County of Toulouse · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>When Eleanor, heiress of William X, married <b>Louis VII</b> in 1137 and Henry Plantagenet in 1152, the duchy passed into the hands of the kings of France and then of England, who governed through seneschals.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Duchy of Aquitaine · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Second Crusade, the armies of <b>Louis VII of France</b> and Conrad III of Germany, with that of the Kingdom of Jerusalem, laid siege to Damascus.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-24">24 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The siege of Damascus by the armies of <b>Louis VII of France</b>, Conrad III of Germany and the Kingdom of Jerusalem failed after four days.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-28">28 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Henry of Anjou, the future Henry II of England, married Eleanor of Aquitaine after the annulment of her marriage to King <b>Louis VII of France</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-18">18 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>King <b>Louis VII of France</b> married Adela of Champagne, his third wife, who became queen of France.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-13">13 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
